WEBVTT

00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:21,880
Do you recommend the use of the low-submide while using iver-vected and fed-bendazole?

00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:25,880
I don't know exactly what you're asking there.

00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:32,880
So, the close-amide is...

00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:35,880
I don't look that up.

00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:39,880
Oh, that's another tapeworm drug.

00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:43,880
Okay, so now I know what the question is.

00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:57,880
So, there is a thought process out there that the reason why iver-vected and fed-bendazole and or fed-bendazole work is because...

00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:03,880
for cancer is that because cancer is parasite, of course, that's why it works.

00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:11,880
So, you take an antiparasitic medication and it's going to kill the cancer.

00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:15,880
I totally disagree with that line of thinking.

00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:20,880
I don't think that's why iver-vected and fed-bendazole work.

00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:26,880
The close-amide is another drug that works on tapeworms where iver-vected and fed-bendazole do not work on tapeworms.

00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:32,880
They work on roundworms and somewhat on liver flukes.

00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:36,880
But the close-amide is going to work on tapeworms.

00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:42,880
So, that's the theory as well to the close-amide also because then we're going to hit all the parasites.

00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:45,880
We totally don't agree with that.

00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:53,880
And the reason is that studies on iver-vected and fed-bendazole and why it tends to work with cancer...

00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:58,880
There's multiple things, but it's not because of its antiparasitic properties.

00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:07,880
The main reason why I believe that those work with cancer is they up-regulate the P53 genetic pathway.

00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:13,880
So, remember your genetics. The P53 pathway is a tumor suppressor pathway.

00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:22,880
So, remember you have all these different tumor suppressor genes that turn on if you have a cell that goes in rapid replication.

00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:33,880
So, you've heard of the BRCA genes. All the BRCA genes are tumor suppressor genes, particularly in the breast and the ovaries.

00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:36,880
People with BRCA gene defects.

00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:48,880
If a cell goes into rapid replication, the defects prevent the BRCA tumor suppressor system from destroying that cell.

00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:55,880
And then the cell can continue to replicate thereby finally giving you a diagnosis of cancer.

00:02:55,880 --> 00:03:03,880
Now, there are other tumor suppressor genes. The P53 is actually the most prominent tumor suppressor gene.

00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:16,880
If one has defects in their P53 system, they're going to have a more difficult time destroying cancer for the inside out, what's called the intrinsic apoptotic pathway.

00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:30,880
So, the intrinsic inside the cell apoptotic death cell death pathway is going to be less apt to kill a growing cancer if a person has defects other P53 genes.

00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:33,880
That's why we test for those.

00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:47,880
However, if we up-regulate the P53 system, whether you have defects or not, you're going to have a better chance of that turning on and destroying the cancer.

00:03:47,880 --> 00:03:51,880
So, there's lots of things that up-regulate the P53.

00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:55,880
Tumoric up-regulates the P53 system.

00:03:55,880 --> 00:04:00,880
Green T extract, EGCG, can up-regulate the P53 system.

00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:06,880
Ivermectin and fed mendisol also up-regulate the P53 pathway.

00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:20,880
So, remember, if we can up-regulate tumor suppressor pathways, meaning increase the function of them, then if we can increase the function of them, they have a better chance of destroying the cancer from the inside out,

00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:24,880
via the intrinsic apoptotic pathway.

00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:35,880
So, I do not believe that it's the anti-paracidic properties of Ivermectin and fed mendisol that destroy cancer.

00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:42,880
It's the genetic effect of the genetic P53 pathway that doesn't.

00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:52,880
Now, there's other studies that show other pathways, metabolic pathways, that these also work on helping destroy cancer.

00:04:52,880 --> 00:04:57,880
But there's not really data to say that it has anything to do with parasites.

